From: Nahum Shalman <nshalman@edgecast.io>
Date: Thu, 12 Jun 2026 02:30:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] killUser: treat child SIGKILL exit as success on illumos

killUser forks a child, setuid's to the build user, and calls
kill(-1, SIGKILL) to wipe any stragglers. On Linux this works because
kill(-1, sig) from a non-root process is documented to skip the caller.
On illumos, kill(-1, sig) from a non-root process sends the signal to
all processes owned by that uid *including the caller* — so the child
suicides immediately after the kill returns 0.

The child therefore exits via SIGKILL instead of reaching _exit(0), and
the parent throws "cannot kill processes for uid 'NNN': failed due to
signal 9 (Killed)". This was invisible before because useBuildUsers()
returned false on illumos (patch 0011 fixes that); now any build that
uses build users hits this error during cleanup.

macOS has the same kill(-1) semantics but works around it with
`syscall(SYS_kill, -1, SIGKILL, false)` (third arg false = "don't
follow POSIX, don't kill self"). illumos has no equivalent third arg.

The least-invasive fix: on illumos, accept WIFSIGNALED && WTERMSIG ==
SIGKILL from the child as success. Either every target process got the
kill (good outcome, child died as a side effect of its own request) or
the child got killed externally — but kill(-1, SIGKILL) on uid N is
the only way to deliver SIGKILL to a process running as N from inside
the child, so external SIGKILL is implausible. A proper fix would
enumerate /proc and kill each non-self pid individually; deferred.

---
 src/libutil/unix/processes.cc | 9 +++++++++
 1 file changed, 9 insertions(+)

--- a/src/libutil/unix/processes.cc
+++ b/src/libutil/unix/processes.cc
@@ -151,8 +151,17 @@ void killUser(uid_t uid)
     });

     int status = pid.wait();
+#ifdef __sun
+    /* illumos kill(-1, sig) from a non-root process signals the caller
+       too, so the child suicides successfully and exits via SIGKILL
+       instead of _exit(0). Accept that as the expected outcome. */
+    if (status != 0
+        && !(WIFSIGNALED(status) && WTERMSIG(status) == SIGKILL))
+        throw Error("cannot kill processes for uid '%1%': %2%", uid, statusToString(status));
+#else
     if (status != 0)
         throw Error("cannot kill processes for uid '%1%': %2%", uid, statusToString(status));
+#endif

     /* !!! We should really do some check to make sure that there are
        no processes left running under `uid', but there is no portable
